{:edb/db-before-t 1945 :edb/db-after-t 1946 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535258 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:36:59.329Z" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147149 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{y}{ x^2 } \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $e^{yx}$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147149 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$-\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n3. $e^{yx}$" 13194139535258 false] [17592186147149 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nto each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nabove:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$, then we get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535258 false] [17592186147149 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}.$\n\n First, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}$ to each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}$ above:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ then we get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy},$ then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535258 true] [17592186147150 1100 "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147150 1100 "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0$." 13194139535258 false] [17592186147150 1145 "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2-2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = 2xy+1\n$$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫y^{2} - 2xdx + f(y) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} - x^{2} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫2xy + 1dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} + y + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c\n$$" 13194139535258 false] [17592186147150 1145 "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = y^2-2x$ and $Q(x,y) = 2xy+1.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int {y}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$.  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int 2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147152 1100 "Which of the following differential equations is exact?\n\n1. $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n2. $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n3. $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" 13194139535258 false] [17592186147152 1100 "Which of the following differential equations is exact?  \n\n- $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\n- $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147152 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n$(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy); = 2x$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 4); = 2x$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation I is exact.\n- The second equation is\n$(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 2x); = 2y$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy + 1); = y$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n- The third equation is\n$(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - y); =-1$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y - x); =-1$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535258 false] [17592186147152 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n\n$$\n(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, equation I is exact.\n\n- The second equation is\n\n$$\n(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x) \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+1) \\\\  & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n\n- The third equation is\n\n$$\n(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-x) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535258 true] [17592186147153 1100 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147153 1100 "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n$y' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}$." 13194139535258 false] [17592186147153 1145 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{11 + ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(11 + ye^{xy}) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xe^{xy} - 13y) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫(11 + ye^{xy})dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= 11x + e^{xy} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫(xe^{xy} - 13y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} - \\frac{13}{2}y^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad f(y) =-\\dfrac{13}{2}y^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c\n$$" 13194139535258 false] [17592186147153 1145 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{11+y{e}^{xy}}{13y-x{e}^{xy}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(11+y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}-13y) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n \nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (11+y{e}^{xy})\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =11x+{e}^{xy}+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x{e}^{xy}-13y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}-\\frac{13}{2}{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad  f(y) = - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186147437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340231 13194139535258 true] [17592186147437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340261 13194139535258 true] [17592186147437 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340268 13194139535258 true] [17592186147438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340196 13194139535258 true] [17592186147438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340203 13194139535258 true] [17592186147438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340210 13194139535258 true] [17592186147438 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340349 13194139535258 true] [17592186147439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340217 13194139535258 true] [17592186147439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340224 13194139535258 true] [17592186147439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340335 13194139535258 true] [17592186147439 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340342 13194139535258 true] [17592186147440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340300 13194139535258 true] [17592186147440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340307 13194139535258 true] [17592186147440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340314 13194139535258 true] [17592186147440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340321 13194139535258 true] [17592186147440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340328 13194139535258 true] [17592186148075 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535258 true] [17592186148075 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\n- $2x+7$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186148075 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$" 13194139535258 false] [17592186148075 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." 13194139535258 true] [17592186148076 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535258 true] [17592186148076 1100 "Find the solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$$\n(4+y)\\text{d}x+(x+{e}^{y})\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186148076 1100 "Find the solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(4 + y)dx + (x + e^{y})dy = 0$\n\nthat satisfies the initial condition $y(0) = 1$." 13194139535258 false] [17592186148076 1145 "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=4+y$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{y}.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (4+y)\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =4x+xy+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x+{e}^{y})\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =xy+{e}^{y}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=4x,\\,f(y)={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n4x+xy+{e}^{y}=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$ when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+xy+{e}^{y} & =c \\\\ 4(0)+(0)(1)+{e}^{1} & =c \\\\ c & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n4x+xy+{e}^{y}=e.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340197 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1089 "q-103172" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$\n- $3{y}^{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1113 #uuid "d0e84f03-1d6a-5d04-a139-0cf3f851d753" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340196 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2})=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1))=0=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)$ gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340198 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340199 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340200 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340201 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340202 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1041 "selection" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340198 13194139535258 true] [17592186340197 1110 #uuid "2f23bfff-97f3-5e5d-beb8-38983083c3ca" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340198 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340198 1111 #uuid "47ea3880-97d6-5695-959f-c6691bd2a8be" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340199 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340199 1111 #uuid "8418349a-7031-59ca-9349-9aa519acc2be" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340200 1034 "I only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340200 1111 #uuid "8670f68b-2cbd-50cb-a4c3-411f8bf23928" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340201 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340201 1111 #uuid "9de6bd51-13cf-58cd-806d-a3d3e528aa7b" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340202 1034 "II only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340202 1111 #uuid "bf5fcd89-355f-5aea-8bf6-bead093bda0e" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340204 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1089 "q-103174" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $6x{y}^{2}$\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$\n- $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1113 #uuid "a5f4a045-5343-5261-852f-034babd8107f" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340203 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂x}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6x{y}^{2})=6{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2}=\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\ne \\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''II only''." 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340205 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340206 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340207 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340208 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340209 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1041 "selection" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340209 13194139535258 true] [17592186340204 1110 #uuid "1b757bc7-03fd-5243-90e7-459c930e796f" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340205 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340205 1111 #uuid "3454ba12-5cb8-5dc0-88cf-7685227e3a61" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340206 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340206 1111 #uuid "37d56f4f-789e-5f7d-91ce-c6c312c7c28b" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340207 1034 "I only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340207 1111 #uuid "52cedde0-0bbf-5e75-9b9e-1a91c171b12b" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340208 1034 "III only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340208 1111 #uuid "980a631b-e920-5150-bd25-a05d11769f53" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340209 1034 "II only" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340209 1111 #uuid "c97a74aa-f9ed-59b5-bc0d-a025f921e91b" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340211 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1089 "q-103175" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1100 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$\n- $-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1113 #uuid "368064cc-9b0a-544e-bc34-335b8aa95f98" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340210 1145 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2y\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2}$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''I only''." 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340212 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340213 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340214 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340215 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340216 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1041 "selection" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340214 13194139535258 true] [17592186340211 1110 #uuid "7d81e09e-2d7a-590b-af64-82f21f2a36ba" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340212 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535258 true] [17592186340212 1111 #uuid "69da71c6-0818-56c1-be1b-d88e3521bc19" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340213 1034 "II and III