general solution of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition $y(0)=1.$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340300 1113 #uuid "806a537b-2d39-5ccf-b479-d935eaaf7929" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340300 1145 "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nStarting from \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3}{\\,2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by$(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(2y-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,\\text{d}y=(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3,\\,Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\\,✓\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂u}{∂x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+3)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+3x+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=3x,\\,f(y)=-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=1$  when $x=0.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x & =c \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot 1)-(1)^{2}+3\\cdot 0 & =c \\\\ c & =-1-1 \\\\ c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{y}^{2}+3x=-2.\n$$" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340302 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340303 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340304 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340305 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340306 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1041 "selection" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340304 13194139535258 true] [17592186340301 1110 #uuid "871a2ced-dbfb-578c-849e-d5a1208fba1e" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340302 1034 "{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-3x=0" 13194139535258 true] [17592186340302 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535258 true] [17592186340302 1111 #uuid "6560f956-6faf-516d-b75d-a14cdbe43d27" 13194139535258 t